{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NbbnGznd8kDgVLDEUWEJgz","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — xling_calib_direction\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: **EACL 2027** (ARR submission deadline: August 3, 2026 — 10.5 weeks from project start)\n5\t- Core submission: 2 models × 20 languages × all contributions\n6\t- Camera-ready extension: Gemma 4 31B Dense + 30+ languages\n7\t\n8\t## Core Insight\n9\tThe model's internal correctness direction occupies a language-agnostic low-dimensional subspace that persists even as global representations specialize by language (CKA declines with depth). A Simpson's Paradox reveals the true CKA-transfer relationship: raw correlation is negative (depth confound), but controlling for depth yields a significant positive partial correlation — within each layer, higher cross-lingual CKA predicts better transfer. This geometric insight, combined with the architecture-dependent depth-transferability tradeoff (strong on Qwen, weak on Llama), provides a structural explanation for when and why cross-lingual probe transfer succeeds or fails. CaCLR tests whether distributional matching can shift this frontier without target-language labels.\n10\t\n11\t## Core Claims (4, causal chain)\n12\t\n13\t### Claim 1 — Pareto Frontier (Discovery)\n14\tCross-lingual correctness probes reveal a depth-transferability tradeoff that varies across architectures: on some architectures (Qwen), deeper layers produce better English probes but consistently degrade in cross-lingual transfer (strong tradeoff); on others (Llama), the tradeoff is language-specific — some languages improve with depth while others plateau or degrade. Architecture variation in tradeoff shape is an observational finding (n=2-3 architectures differing in type, scale, layer count, and training data; no causal attribution to specific architectural features like MoE routing without direct evidence).\n15\t- MVP evidence: Qwen2.5-7B shows clear Pareto (L27 EN best but transfer drops 2-4pp vs L24 across 4/5 langs). Llama-3.1-8B shows weak Pareto (only 2/5 langs show depth-transfer tradeoff; ar/ko improve with depth).\n16\t- Full evidence: 2 large architectures × 20 languages × all layers × 5 transfer conditions. Per-language Spearman rho to quantify Pareto strength per language-architecture combination.\n17\t\n18\t### Claim 2 — Decomposition (Analysis)\n19\tCross-lingual correctness transfer decomposes into a difficulty-shared component (language-agnostic, transferable at all depths) and a capability-specific component (language-dependent, driving deep-layer transfer degradation). This decomposition is prescriptive: it predicts which alignment strategy is needed per language-layer combination.\n20\t- Evidence: IRT difficulty probes → project out → retrain → compare transfer.\n21\t\n22\t### Claim 3 — CaCLR (Method / Diagnostic)\n23\tCaCLR (L_BCE + α·L_SB-ECE + β·L_consistency, where L_consistency uses MMD on probe logits computed from parallel inputs — English and target language versions of the same GlobalMMLU train-split items) tests whether joint calibration + cross-lingual invariance optimization can shift the Pareto frontier without target-language correctness labels. Note: MMD must operate on probe outputs (logits), not raw activations, to ensure gradient flow to probe parameters (M007). For linear probes, logits are 1-D, so MMD operates in 1-D space — this is a design limitation worth discussing.\n24\t- **If consistency loss helps**: CaCLR is a method contribution that enables deeper, better-calibrated probe transfer.\n25\t- **If consistency loss is inert**: The negative result itself is the contribution — demonstrating that MMD-based distributional matching fails for correctness probe transfer (scope to MMD specifically; acknowledge domain adaptation theory predicts marginal matching failure when conditional distributions differ, cite Zhao et al. 2019). CKA geometric analysis reveals that correctness information occupies a low-dimensional language-agnostic subspace that persists even as global representations diverge (CKA drops). MMD matches marginal distributions but cannot align low-dimensional directional structure within those distributions. Linear projection succeeds because it learns the subspace rotation directly. This closes the MMD distributional-matching path with both empirical evidence and geometric analysis. **Framing note**: CaCLR is presented as the natural baseline that tests whether distributional matching works for this problem class — NOT as a novel method contribution. Acknowledge that this is a self-proposed method; the contribution is the systematic negative result + geometric explanation, not the method itself.\n26\t- **Implementation specification (MUST report for reproducibility)**: MMD kernel (Gaussian RBF with median heuristic bandwidth), optimizer (Adam), learning rate, epochs, early stopping criterion, initialization strategy (from standard LR solution vs random), batch size, whether MMD computed on same items as BCE or held-out. For negative result, higher reproducibility bar — community must verify fair trial.\n27\t- Parallel activations source: GlobalMMLU test split, partitioned into probe_train (70%) and eval (30%) via stratified split. English BCE labels and parallel activations for MMD both come from probe_train portion only; evaluation exclusively on eval portion. This avoids same-data-for-training-and-evaluation concern while requiring zero target-language *labels*. GlobalMMLU has no train split (only test+dev); dev (285 samples) too small for MMD. Report parallel set size per language and translation quality assessment for low-resource languages.\n28\t- MVP evidence: Mean-shift fails (unreliable across 5 languages) — motivates principled approach.\n29\t- Evidence: CaCLR vs zero-shot/mean-shift/linear-projection/few-shot across full Pareto frontier. α sensitivity sweep required. β sweep across 3 OOM to test consistency loss value.\n30\t\n31\t### Claim 4 — Cross-Lingual Orthogonality (Supporting Evidence, subsection not standalone claim)\n32\tThe correctness-confidence orthogonality (Miao & Ungar, 2026; cosine < 0.04) persists across languages but varies with resource tier and architecture. **Downgraded from standalone claim to supporting evidence** (D012): reviewer assessment indicates weakest standalone novelty (\"method replication to more languages\"). Presented as subsection supporting Claim 1's architecture/language analysis. Space freed for stronger contributions (control task finding, projection sample efficiency).\n33\t- Evidence: Cosine similarity measurement in 20 languages × 2+ architectures.\n34\t\n35\t### Key Empirical Contributions (elevated from appendix to main body, D012)\n36\t- **Control task methodological warning**: Hewitt & Liang selectivity analysis reveals ~28% of low-resource language-layer probing results are capacity artifacts (selectivity below meaningful threshold). First application of probing controls to cross-lingual transfer setting. Field-level implication: all cross-lingual probing studies should include selectivity checks.\n37\t- **Linear projection sample efficiency**: Procrustes alignment with ~50 parallel pairs achieves 98% of 200-pair performance. Resource-tier stratification: HIGH languages saturate at ~22 pairs, LOW at ~48. Directly actionable for practitioners.\n38\t- **Procrustes effect reversal**: Linear projection helps when zero-shot AUROC < 0.50 but hurts when > 0.60. Implies alignment strategy must be conditioned on baseline transfer quality — not all languages benefit from geometric alignment.\n39\t\n40\t## Story Spine\n41\t- **Problem**: Non-English languages suffer 5x worse calibration (ECE) in multilingual LLMs. Output-based calibration is fundamentally limited — correctness and verbalized confidence directions are orthogonal. Existing fixes require per-language data (LACE) or 5x inference cost (MKA).\n42\t- **Gaps**: All probe-based calibration methods (CORAL, CCPS, Confidence Manifold, Miao & Ungar steering) are English-only. Nobody has tested whether this research line works multilingually. Xiao et al. transferred knowledge-boundary probes (input-level answerability), not correctness probes (output-dependent accuracy) — fundamentally different targets.\n43\t- **Our framing**: First large-scale study comparing correctness probe transfer across architectures in multilingual settings. Core novel findings: (1) Simpson's Paradox in CKA-transfer relationship — raw correlation negative (depth confound), partial correlation positive (within-layer, higher CKA = better transfer) — a genuine geometric insight not predicted by prior work; (2) ~28% of low-resource probing results are capacity artifacts (control task warning for the field); (3) linear projection with ~50 pairs is the practical solution, with resource-tier-dependent saturation. We discover the depth-transferability tradeoff, decompose transfer into difficulty/capability components, test CaCLR as the natural distributional-matching baseline, and provide CKA geometric analysis of why transfer succeeds or fails. This is both a field-level viability test and a source of novel methodological insights.\n44\t- **Headline template (method success)**: \"On GlobalMMLU across 20 languages, CaCLR-trained correctness probes reduce non-English ECE by X% compared to temperature scaling, requiring zero target-language correctness labels.\"\n45\t- **Headline template (negative result)**: \"Cross-lingual correctness probe transfer reveals architecture-dependent Pareto frontier; the natural distributional-matching approach (CaCLR) fails, but simple linear projection with ~50 parallel pairs matches supervised baselines.\"\n46\t- **Constructive path forward**: For languages where zero-shot transfer fails, the paper identifies three viable directions: (1) language-specific probes with minimal labeled data (few-shot 25-100 shown effective), (2) adapter-based alignment leveraging pretrained multilingual adapters, (3) multilingual probe training on pooled high-resource languages.\n47\t\n48\t## Field-Level Significance\n49\tThis paper tests whether the entire probe-based calibration research line works beyond English. Regardless of results, it answers a field-level question about multilingual viability of representation-level calibration — validating or closing this path for the community.\n50\t\n51\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Responses\n52\t1. **\"Xiao et al. already did this\"** → Knowledge boundary (input-level, binary answerability) vs correctness (output-dependent accuracy). They measure probing accuracy, not calibration. 8 languages vs our 20+. No Pareto frontier. We explicitly analyze why answerability transfers (Xiao's partial success) but correctness does not — different signal types have different cross-lingual alignment properties.\n53\t2. **\"Temperature scaling baseline\"** → We compare against it. CaCLR optimizes during training, not post-hoc. Temperature scaling can't address cross-lingual representation misalignment.\n54\t3. **\"Mean-shift too weak / why does it fail?\"** → We provide a formal proof: under StandardScaler + logistic regression, mean-shift adds a constant offset to all logits → AUROC is rank-invariant → ΔAUROC ≡ 0 mathematically. MVP data confirms: ΔAUROC = 0.000000 across all 5 languages × all layers. ECE changes are unreliable because they depend on threshold-sensitive binning of shifted probabilities. This proof (1-2 paragraphs + experimental validation) distinguishes us from Xiao et al. (where mean-shift works under different metrics/pipeline) and directly motivates why stronger alignment methods (linear projection, CaCLR) are needed.\n55\t4. **\"Difficulty confound\"** → Claim 2's decomposition ablation explicitly controls for this via IRT difficulty probes.\n56\t5. **\"INT8 artifacts\"** → Validation ablation comparing INT8 vs FP16 at representative layers.\n57\t6. **\"Only MCQA\"** → Acknowledged. MCQA enables clean correctness labeling and parallel multilingual evaluation via GlobalMMLU.\n58\t7. **\"Predictable from domain adaptation theory\"** → We explicitly derive predictions from Ben-David et al. (2010) H-divergence framework and test them. The resource-tier gradient is expected; the novel findings are (a) the Pareto frontier shape is architecture-dependent (not predicted by theory), (b) difficulty-capability entanglement, and (c) distributional matching (CaCLR) fails despite seemingly matching the theory's prescription.\n59\t8. **\"No statistical tests\"** → All key comparisons include bootstrap CIs (1000 resamples) and paired permutation tests across languages. Effect sizes reported with Cohen's d.\n60\t9. **\"Probing methodology concerns\"** → We include Hewitt & Liang (2019) control tasks to validate that probe AUROC reflects genuine correctness directions, not dataset artifacts.\n61\t10. **\"No mechanistic explanation\"** → CKA geometric analysis reveals a Simpson's Paradox: raw CKA-transfer correlation is negative (depth confound), but partial correlation controlling for depth is significantly positive (Llama pilot: rho=+0.41, p=0.006; 14/17 layer-level correlations positive). Within each layer, higher cross-lingual CKA predicts better transfer — CKA provides genuine explanatory power beyond depth. Subspace angle analysis (quantitative tables) strengthens geometric narrative. Frame as \"geometric analysis\" not \"mechanistic explanation.\" **Transparency requirements**: (a) Report Qwen pilot non-significant (rho=+0.18, p=0.274) — may be architecture-dependent or underpowered; (b) Bootstrap CIs (1000 resamples) for all partial correlations; (c) Discuss potential tautology — CKA and transfer AUROC both measure EN-target similarity, but Simpson's Paradox sign reversal is non-trivial and not predicted by measurement overlap alone.\n62\t11. **\"Why not conformal prediction?\"** → Discussed in Related Work. Conformal prediction provides distribution-free coverage guarantees but requires calibration data per language; our approach targets zero-label transfer. Complementary, not competing.\n63\t12. **\"CaCLR parallel activations = test leakage?\"** → Parallel activations are from GlobalMMLU train split only, never test items. The requirement is parallel inputs (available via existing translations), not target-language labels.\n64\t13. **\"CaCLR is your own method — self-debunking is less impactful\"** → CaCLR is framed as the natural baseline that tests the distributional-matching hypothesis for this problem class, not as a novel method. The contribution is the systematic negative result (β sweep across 3 OOM, component ablation) + geometric explanation of why it fails, which saves the community from pursuing this path. The positive contribution is identifying linear projection (~50 pairs) as the practical solution.\n65\t14. **\"CKA is correlational, not mechanistic\"** → We agree and frame as geometric/correlational. MVP pilot reveals Simpson's Paradox: raw correlation negative (depth confound), partial correlation positive and significant. CKA adds genuine explanatory power beyond depth. Full-scale analysis (20 languages) with bootstrap CIs will confirm. This sign reversal is itself a novel methodological finding for the cross-lingual probing literature.\n66\t\n67\t## Additional Related Work Coverage (Dream-identified gaps)\n68\t- **Domain adaptation theory**: Ben-David et al. (2010) H-divergence provides theoretical grounding for resource-tier gradient. Zhao et al. (2019) \"On learning invariant representations for domain adaptation\" — directly explains why marginal distribution matching fails when conditional distributions differ; stronger theoretical grounding for CaCLR negative result than Ben-David alone.\n69\t- **Cross-lingual alignment**: MUSE (Conneau et al. 2018), VecMap (Artetxe et al. 2018) — linear projection's effectiveness is consistent with this literature. Explicitly discuss relationship: our linear projection is conceptually VecMap/MUSE applied at sentence-level correctness representations; the contribution is demonstrating this works for correctness probing and characterizing sample efficiency, not the projection technique itself.\n70\t- **Representation similarity**: CKA (Kornblith et al. 2019), SVCCA (Raghu et al. 2017) — CKA analysis reveals correctness direction persists as low-dimensional subspace despite global representation divergence. Must report partial correlation controlling for depth.\n71\t- **Probing methodology**: Hewitt & Liang (2019) control tasks, Voita & Titov (2020) MDL probes — we include control tasks to validate probe selectivity. Control task finding (low-resource capacity artifacts) is itself a contribution.\n72\t- **Conformal prediction**: Angelopoulos & Bates (2023) — complementary approach discussed in Related Work; requires per-language calibration data vs our zero-label target.\n73\t- **Cross-lingual transfer learning**: Pires et al. (2019) multilingual BERT, Wu & Dredze (2019) — broader context for cross-lingual transfer degradation with language distance.\n74\t- **DALI**: Ravisankar et al. (2026) — cross-lingual representation alignment; must cite if published.\n75\t\n76\t## Differentiation from Closest Prior Work\n77\t| Paper | Their Focus | Our Difference |\n78\t|-------|-------------|----------------|\n79\t| Xiao et al. (ACL 2025) | Knowledge boundary cross-lingual probe transfer | Correctness (not boundary); calibration metrics (not probing accuracy); 20+ languages; Pareto frontier. We analyze WHY answerability transfers but correctness does not. |\n80\t| LACE (Zhou et al.) | Per-language layer ensemble | Zero target-language labels; directional probe transfer; CaCLR method |\n81\t| CORAL (Miao et al.) | Cross-task English correctness probes | Cross-lingual (not cross-task); CaCLR solves their identified distribution-shift problem |\n82\t| Miao & Ungar (2026) | Correctness-confidence orthogonality + steering | English-only 7-8B → multilingual multi-architecture; first non-English orthogonality measurement |\n83\t| Civelli et al. | Cross-lingual difficulty probe transfer | Correctness (not difficulty); difficulty is their control for our ablation |\n84\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"93fd10ce-8877-4672-be96-d4a16ab6d261","uuid":"7203da28-cb02-4bd5-84de-75729aff38ea","timestamp":"2026-05-21T05:29:15.997Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519_2/projects/xling_calib_direction/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T044911/best/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — xling_calib_direction\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: **EACL 2027** (ARR submission deadline: August 3, 2026 — 10.5 weeks from project start)\n- Core submission: 2 models × 20 languages × all contributions\n- Camera-ready extension: Gemma 4 31B Dense + 30+ languages\n\n## Core Insight\nThe model's internal correctness direction occupies a language-agnostic low-dimensional subspace that persists even as global representations specialize by language (CKA declines with depth). A Simpson's Paradox reveals the true CKA-transfer relationship: raw correlation is negative (depth confound), but controlling for depth yields a significant positive partial correlation — within each layer, higher cross-lingual CKA predicts better transfer. This geometric insight, combined with the architecture-dependent depth-transferability tradeoff (strong on Qwen, weak on Llama), provides a structural explanation for when and why cross-lingual probe transfer succeeds or fails. CaCLR tests whether distributional matching can shift this frontier without target-language labels.\n\n## Core Claims (4, causal chain)\n\n### Claim 1 — Pareto Frontier (Discovery)\nCross-lingual correctness probes reveal a depth-transferability tradeoff that varies across architectures: on some architectures (Qwen), deeper layers produce better English probes but consistently degrade in cross-lingual transfer (strong tradeoff); on others (Llama), the tradeoff is language-specific — some languages improve with depth while others plateau or degrade. Architecture variation in tradeoff shape is an observational finding (n=2-3 architectures differing in type, scale, layer count, and training data; no causal attribution to specific architectural features like MoE routing without direct evidence).\n- MVP evidence: Qwen2.5-7B shows clear Pareto (L27 EN best but transfer drops 2-4pp vs L24 across 4/5 langs). Llama-3.1-8B shows weak Pareto (only 2/5 langs show depth-transfer tradeoff; ar/ko improve with depth).\n- Full evidence: 2 large architectures × 20 languages × all layers × 5 transfer conditions. Per-language Spearman rho to quantify Pareto strength per language-architecture combination.\n\n### Claim 2 — Decomposition (Analysis)\nCross-lingual correctness transfer decomposes into a difficulty-shared component (language-agnostic, transferable at all depths) and a capability-specific component (language-dependent, driving deep-layer transfer degradation). This decomposition is prescriptive: it predicts which alignment strategy is needed per language-layer combination.\n- Evidence: IRT difficulty probes → project out → retrain → compare transfer.\n\n### Claim 3 — CaCLR (Method / Diagnostic)\nCaCLR (L_BCE + α·L_SB-ECE + β·L_consistency, where L_consistency uses MMD on probe logits computed from parallel inputs — English and target language versions of the same GlobalMMLU train-split items) tests whether joint calibration + cross-lingual invariance optimization can shift the Pareto frontier without target-language correctness labels. Note: MMD must operate on probe outputs (logits), not raw activations, to ensure gradient flow to probe parameters (M007). For linear probes, logits are 1-D, so MMD operates in 1-D space — this is a design limitation worth discussing.\n- **If consistency loss helps**: CaCLR is a method contribution that enables deeper, better-calibrated probe transfer.\n- **If consistency loss is inert**: The negative result itself is the contribution — demonstrating that MMD-based distributional matching fails for correctness probe transfer (scope to MMD specifically; acknowledge domain adaptation theory predicts marginal matching failure when conditional distributions differ, cite Zhao et al. 2019). CKA geometric analysis reveals that correctness information occupies a low-dimensional language-agnostic subspace that persists even as global representations diverge (CKA drops). MMD matches marginal distributions but cannot align low-dimensional directional structure within those distributions. Linear projection succeeds because it learns the subspace rotation directly. This closes the MMD distributional-matching path with both empirical evidence and geometric analysis. **Framing note**: CaCLR is presented as the natural baseline that tests whether distributional matching works for this problem class — NOT as a novel method contribution. Acknowledge that this is a self-proposed method; the contribution is the systematic negative result + geometric explanation, not the method itself.\n- **Implementation specification (MUST report for reproducibility)**: MMD kernel (Gaussian RBF with median heuristic bandwidth), optimizer (Adam), learning rate, epochs, early stopping criterion, initialization strategy (from standard LR solution vs random), batch size, whether MMD computed on same items as BCE or held-out. For negative result, higher reproducibility bar — community must verify fair trial.\n- Parallel activations source: GlobalMMLU test split, partitioned into probe_train (70%) and eval (30%) via stratified split. English BCE labels and parallel activations for MMD both come from probe_train portion only; evaluation exclusively on eval portion. This avoids same-data-for-training-and-evaluation concern while requiring zero target-language *labels*. GlobalMMLU has no train split (only test+dev); dev (285 samples) too small for MMD. Report parallel set size per language and translation quality assessment for low-resource languages.\n- MVP evidence: Mean-shift fails (unreliable across 5 languages) — motivates principled approach.\n- Evidence: CaCLR vs zero-shot/mean-shift/linear-projection/few-shot across full Pareto frontier. α sensitivity sweep required. β sweep across 3 OOM to test consistency loss value.\n\n### Claim 4 — Cross-Lingual Orthogonality (Supporting Evidence, subsection not standalone claim)\nThe correctness-confidence orthogonality (Miao & Ungar, 2026; cosine < 0.04) persists across languages but varies with resource tier and architecture. **Downgraded from standalone claim to supporting evidence** (D012): reviewer assessment indicates weakest standalone novelty (\"method replication to more languages\"). Presented as subsection supporting Claim 1's architecture/language analysis. Space freed for stronger contributions (control task finding, projection sample efficiency).\n- Evidence: Cosine similarity measurement in 20 languages × 2+ architectures.\n\n### Key Empirical Contributions (elevated from appendix to main body, D012)\n- **Control task methodological warning**: Hewitt & Liang selectivity analysis reveals ~28% of low-resource language-layer probing results are capacity artifacts (selectivity below meaningful threshold). First application of probing controls to cross-lingual transfer setting. Field-level implication: all cross-lingual probing studies should include selectivity checks.\n- **Linear projection sample efficiency**: Procrustes alignment with ~50 parallel pairs achieves 98% of 200-pair performance. Resource-tier stratification: HIGH languages saturate at ~22 pairs, LOW at ~48. Directly actionable for practitioners.\n- **Procrustes effect reversal**: Linear projection helps when zero-shot AUROC < 0.50 but hurts when > 0.60. Implies alignment strategy must be conditioned on baseline transfer quality — not all languages benefit from geometric alignment.\n\n## Story Spine\n- **Problem**: Non-English languages suffer 5x worse calibration (ECE) in multilingual LLMs. Output-based calibration is fundamentally limited — correctness and verbalized confidence directions are orthogonal. Existing fixes require per-language data (LACE) or 5x inference cost (MKA).\n- **Gaps**: All probe-based calibration methods (CORAL, CCPS, Confidence Manifold, Miao & Ungar steering) are English-only. Nobody has tested whether this research line works multilingually. Xiao et al. transferred knowledge-boundary probes (input-level answerability), not correctness probes (output-dependent accuracy) — fundamentally different targets.\n- **Our framing**: First large-scale study comparing correctness probe transfer across architectures in multilingual settings. Core novel findings: (1) Simpson's Paradox in CKA-transfer relationship — raw correlation negative (depth confound), partial correlation positive (within-layer, higher CKA = better transfer) — a genuine geometric insight not predicted by prior work; (2) ~28% of low-resource probing results are capacity artifacts (control task warning for the field); (3) linear projection with ~50 pairs is the practical solution, with resource-tier-dependent saturation. We discover the depth-transferability tradeoff, decompose transfer into difficulty/capability components, test CaCLR as the natural distributional-matching baseline, and provide CKA geometric analysis of why transfer succeeds or fails. This is both a field-level viability test and a source of novel methodological insights.\n- **Headline template (method success)**: \"On GlobalMMLU across 20 languages, CaCLR-trained correctness probes reduce non-English ECE by X% compared to temperature scaling, requiring zero target-language correctness labels.\"\n- **Headline template (negative result)**: \"Cross-lingual correctness probe transfer reveals architecture-dependent Pareto frontier; the natural distributional-matching approach (CaCLR) fails, but simple linear projection with ~50 parallel pairs matches supervised baselines.\"\n- **Constructive path forward**: For languages where zero-shot transfer fails, the paper identifies three viable directions: (1) language-specific probes with minimal labeled data (few-shot 25-100 shown effective), (2) adapter-based alignment leveraging pretrained multilingual adapters, (3) multilingual probe training on pooled high-resource languages.\n\n## Field-Level Significance\nThis paper tests whether the entire probe-based calibration research line works beyond English. Regardless of results, it answers a field-level question about multilingual viability of representation-level calibration — validating or closing this path for the community.\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Responses\n1. **\"Xiao et al. already did this\"** → Knowledge boundary (input-level, binary answerability) vs correctness (output-dependent accuracy). They measure probing accuracy, not calibration. 8 languages vs our 20+. No Pareto frontier. We explicitly analyze why answerability transfers (Xiao's partial success) but correctness does not — different signal types have different cross-lingual alignment properties.\n2. **\"Temperature scaling baseline\"** → We compare against it. CaCLR optimizes during training, not post-hoc. Temperature scaling can't address cross-lingual representation misalignment.\n3. **\"Mean-shift too weak / why does it fail?\"** → We provide a formal proof: under StandardScaler + logistic regression, mean-shift adds a constant offset to all logits → AUROC is rank-invariant → ΔAUROC ≡ 0 mathematically. MVP data confirms: ΔAUROC = 0.000000 across all 5 languages × all layers. ECE changes are unreliable because they depend on threshold-sensitive binning of shifted probabilities. This proof (1-2 paragraphs + experimental validation) distinguishes us from Xiao et al. (where mean-shift works under different metrics/pipeline) and directly motivates why stronger alignment methods (linear projection, CaCLR) are needed.\n4. **\"Difficulty confound\"** → Claim 2's decomposition ablation explicitly controls for this via IRT difficulty probes.\n5. **\"INT8 artifacts\"** → Validation ablation comparing INT8 vs FP16 at representative layers.\n6. **\"Only MCQA\"** → Acknowledged. MCQA enables clean correctness labeling and parallel multilingual evaluation via GlobalMMLU.\n7. **\"Predictable from domain adaptation theory\"** → We explicitly derive predictions from Ben-David et al. (2010) H-divergence framework and test them. The resource-tier gradient is expected; the novel findings are (a) the Pareto frontier shape is architecture-dependent (not predicted by theory), (b) difficulty-capability entanglement, and (c) distributional matching (CaCLR) fails despite seemingly matching the theory's prescription.\n8. **\"No statistical tests\"** → All key comparisons include bootstrap CIs (1000 resamples) and paired permutation tests across languages. Effect sizes reported with Cohen's d.\n9. **\"Probing methodology concerns\"** → We include Hewitt & Liang (2019) control tasks to validate that probe AUROC reflects genuine correctness directions, not dataset artifacts.\n10. **\"No mechanistic explanation\"** → CKA geometric analysis reveals a Simpson's Paradox: raw CKA-transfer correlation is negative (depth confound), but partial correlation controlling for depth is significantly positive (Llama pilot: rho=+0.41, p=0.006; 14/17 layer-level correlations positive). Within each layer, higher cross-lingual CKA predicts better transfer — CKA provides genuine explanatory power beyond depth. Subspace angle analysis (quantitative tables) strengthens geometric narrative. Frame as \"geometric analysis\" not \"mechanistic explanation.\" **Transparency requirements**: (a) Report Qwen pilot non-significant (rho=+0.18, p=0.274) — may be architecture-dependent or underpowered; (b) Bootstrap CIs (1000 resamples) for all partial correlations; (c) Discuss potential tautology — CKA and transfer AUROC both measure EN-target similarity, but Simpson's Paradox sign reversal is non-trivial and not predicted by measurement overlap alone.\n11. **\"Why not conformal prediction?\"** → Discussed in Related Work. Conformal prediction provides distribution-free coverage guarantees but requires calibration data per language; our approach targets zero-label transfer. Complementary, not competing.\n12. **\"CaCLR parallel activations = test leakage?\"** → Parallel activations are from GlobalMMLU train split only, never test items. The requirement is parallel inputs (available via existing translations), not target-language labels.\n13. **\"CaCLR is your own method — self-debunking is less impactful\"** → CaCLR is framed as the natural baseline that tests the distributional-matching hypothesis for this problem class, not as a novel method. The contribution is the systematic negative result (β sweep across 3 OOM, component ablation) + geometric explanation of why it fails, which saves the community from pursuing this path. The positive contribution is identifying linear projection (~50 pairs) as the practical solution.\n14. **\"CKA is correlational, not mechanistic\"** → We agree and frame as geometric/correlational. MVP pilot reveals Simpson's Paradox: raw correlation negative (depth confound), partial correlation positive and significant. CKA adds genuine explanatory power beyond depth. Full-scale analysis (20 languages) with bootstrap CIs will confirm. This sign reversal is itself a novel methodological finding for the cross-lingual probing literature.\n\n## Additional Related Work Coverage (Dream-identified gaps)\n- **Domain adaptation theory**: Ben-David et al. (2010) H-divergence provides theoretical grounding for resource-tier gradient. Zhao et al. (2019) \"On learning invariant representations for domain adaptation\" — directly explains why marginal distribution matching fails when conditional distributions differ; stronger theoretical grounding for CaCLR negative result than Ben-David alone.\n- **Cross-lingual alignment**: MUSE (Conneau et al. 2018), VecMap (Artetxe et al. 2018) — linear projection's effectiveness is consistent with this literature. Explicitly discuss relationship: our linear projection is conceptually VecMap/MUSE applied at sentence-level correctness representations; the contribution is demonstrating this works for correctness probing and characterizing sample efficiency, not the projection technique itself.\n- **Representation similarity**: CKA (Kornblith et al. 2019), SVCCA (Raghu et al. 2017) — CKA analysis reveals correctness direction persists as low-dimensional subspace despite global representation divergence. Must report partial correlation controlling for depth.\n- **Probing methodology**: Hewitt & Liang (2019) control tasks, Voita & Titov (2020) MDL probes — we include control tasks to validate probe selectivity. Control task finding (low-resource capacity artifacts) is itself a contribution.\n- **Conformal prediction**: Angelopoulos & Bates (2023) — complementary approach discussed in Related Work; requires per-language calibration data vs our zero-label target.\n- **Cross-lingual transfer learning**: Pires et al. (2019) multilingual BERT, Wu & Dredze (2019) — broader context for cross-lingual transfer degradation with language distance.\n- **DALI**: Ravisankar et al. (2026) — cross-lingual representation alignment; must cite if published.\n\n## Differentiation from Closest Prior Work\n| Paper | Their Focus | Our Difference |\n|-------|-------------|----------------|\n| Xiao et al. (ACL 2025) | Knowledge boundary cross-lingual probe transfer | Correctness (not boundary); calibration metrics (not probing accuracy); 20+ languages; Pareto frontier. We analyze WHY answerability transfers but correctness does not. |\n| LACE (Zhou et al.) | Per-language layer ensemble | Zero target-language labels; directional probe transfer; CaCLR method |\n| CORAL (Miao et al.) | Cross-task English correctness probes | Cross-lingual (not cross-task); CaCLR solves their identified distribution-shift problem |\n| Miao & Ungar (2026) | Correctness-confidence orthogonality + steering | English-only 7-8B → multilingual multi-architecture; first non-English orthogonality measurement |\n| Civelli et al. | Cross-lingual difficulty probe transfer | Correctness (not difficulty); difficulty is their control for our ablation |\n","numLines":84,"startLine":1,"totalLines":84}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GHV62fTB3ahnsfgiuk5QxX","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"Full Pareto Frontier — Qwen3-235B-A22B\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"94-layer MoE (INT4, ~22B active), 24 probed layers (every 4th), 20 typologically diverse languages, English logistic regression probes, 5 transfer conditions: zero-shot, mean-shift, linear projection (200 pairs), few-shot 25/100, CaCLR。重点验证 MVP 发现的 strong depth-transferability tradeoff 是否在大模型上保持。\\n\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"en_best_auroc\": 0.81,\n7\t      \"en_best_layer_idx\": 72,\n8\t      \"en_auroc_range\": [\n9\t        0.58,\n10\t        0.81\n11\t      ],\n12\t      \"en_ece_raw_best\": 0.168,\n13\t      \"zs_mean_auroc_20lang_best_transfer_layer\": 0.72,\n14\t      \"zs_best_transfer_layer_idx\": 52,\n15\t      \"zs_pass_rate_20lang\": 0.9,\n16\t      \"zs_fail_langs\": [\n17\t        \"yo\",\n18\t        \"te\"\n19\t      ],\n20\t      \"zs_auroc_by_tier\": {\n21\t        \"HIGH_mean\": 0.76,\n22\t        \"MID_mean\": 0.72,\n23\t        \"LOW_mean\": 0.63\n24\t      },\n25\t      \"zs_auroc_selected\": {\n26\t        \"zh\": 0.77,\n27\t        \"es\": 0.76,\n28\t        \"fr\": 0.76,\n29\t        \"de\": 0.77,\n30\t        \"ja\": 0.75,\n31\t        \"nl\": 0.76,\n32\t        \"pt\": 0.75,\n33\t        \"it\": 0.75,\n34\t        \"ar\": 0.73,\n35\t        \"hi\": 0.71,\n36\t        \"ko\": 0.72,\n37\t        \"tr\": 0.71,\n38\t        \"ru\": 0.74,\n39\t        \"vi\": 0.7,\n40\t        \"th\": 0.69,\n41\t        \"sw\": 0.63,\n42\t        \"bn\": 0.65,\n43\t        \"yo\": 0.57,\n44\t        \"ig\": 0.61,\n45\t        \"te\": 0.58\n46\t      },\n47\t      \"per_lang_spearman_rho_median\": -0.64,\n48\t      \"per_lang_significant_at_005\": 15,\n49\t      \"global_spearman_rho\": -0.91,\n50\t      \"global_spearman_p\": 0.0001,\n51\t      \"pareto_frontier_layers\": [\n52\t        40,\n53\t        44,\n54\t        